diagnosis_text:
No split observed. End-to-end data-plane connectivity between the OSPF side (20.x) and the EIGRP side (120.x) is intact. Cross-reachability test shows pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10) successfully ping each other in both directions with 0% loss. On the OSPF side, multiple routers (e.g., r1 and r3) have OSPF-learned routes to EIGRP-originated prefixes: r1’s routing table contains 102.1.1.0/24–102.1.4.0/24 via 111.1.1.2 (r11.eth1/B), proving EIGRP→OSPF redistribution is active at the boundary. r11’s FRR config confirms this with “router ospf … redistribute eigrp”.

On the EIGRP side, routers reach OSPF networks using a default toward r11 rather than specific redistributed OSPF routes: r12 has a default route 0.0.0.0/0 via 112.1.1.1 (r11.eth0/EE) and its 120.0.0.0/8 LAN on eth1 (GG), but no explicit 20.0.0.0/8 in EIGRP. r11’s EIGRP process does not have “redistribute ospf” configured, so OSPF→EIGRP redistribution is not occurring, yet connectivity is preserved via the default.

Affected component: none; the boundary redistribution from EIGRP into OSPF is functioning. Mechanism: OSPF learns EIGRP prefixes from r11; EIGRP side uses a default to r11 for OSPF destinations. Localization: r11 (boundary), OSPF on eth1/B (111.1.1.x) and EIGRP on eth0/EE (112.1.1.x). Root cause: There is no outage; two-way redistribution into EIGRP is simply not configured, but traffic still flows via default routing. Corrective fix: If two-way redistribution is desired, add “redistribute ospf” under “router eigrp 100” on r11 with an appropriate EIGRP metric and route policy; otherwise no change is required. 
